The [[W:Early skyscrapers|first skyscrapers]] in [[Balisca]] were built in the years immediately preceding the [[Baliscan Revolution]], beginning as a response to significant overcrowding attributed to population growth, compounded by immigration influxes. The first "true" skyscraper to be constructed in Balisca was the [[Empúries Bank Building]], completed in 1926. 

Skyscrapers in Balisca are amongst the tallest in the [[Western Hemisphere|Western]] and [[Northern Hemisphere|Northern]] hemispheres. A significant number are concentrated in the [[Expansión]] and [[Ampurias]] [[Cantons of Balisca|boroughs]] of the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]]. Since the 1970s, Baliscan cities have embraced an "up, not out" policy, opting to construct taller buildings in close proximity rather than continuing the [[w:Urban sprawl|trend of developing distant suburbs]], which has led to the rise of [[w:Edge city|edge cities]] throughout the country. The subsequent proliferation of multi-tower {{W|public housing estates}}, {{W|mixed-use development|mixed-use}} and {{W|transit-oriented development}}s near transit stations has greatly increased the number of towers nationwide.

{{W|Supertall skyscraper}}s, however, are a relatively new and controversial phenomenon in Balisca. In the thirteen years since the first supertall tower was completed in 1993 ([[333 Trovadores]]), 50,479 of Balisca's 57,681 [[Communes of Balisca|communes]] have either introduced restrictions on supertalls or banned them entirely. Most notably, the Castejón Metropolis introduced a ban on the construction of buildings over the height of {{Convert|400|m|ft|abbr=on}} in 2024, following continued concerns regarding low office occupancy rates and [[Ampurias Tower#Public opposition|the controversy surrounding]] the [[W:List of megatall skyscrapers|megatall]] [[Ampurias Tower]]. Additionally, proposals for large scale building projects have slowed down substantially since the 2010s due to a heightened community awareness of skyscraper's effect on {{W|urban ecology}}, such as changes to air circulation (dubbed as "{{W|wall effect}}") and {{W|light pollution}}.  

As of August 2022, the [[Ampurias Tower]] of Castejón remains the tallest skyscraper in Balisca and [[List of tallest buildings in Abyala|Abyala]] with a height of {{convert|625|m|ft}}. Other ''Castejonero'' skyscrapers, such as [[44 Concordia]], and [[22 Ampuria]] remain some of the tallest buildings in both Balisca and the [[Lurando-Abyala|Algarves]].

The number of skyscrapers in Balisca is slated to grow significantly in the next few years, with over 40 new skyscrapers under construction throughout the country over the height of {{convert|150|m|ft}}, and countless more approved or under consideration. Half of those under construction are slated to be completed before the end of 2026 with all others being completed after that year. 

==Tallest buildings==
This list ranks completed and {{W|topped out}} Balisca's skyscrapers that stand at least {{convert|198|m}} tall based on standard height measurements. This includes spires and architectural details but does not include antenna masts. An equal sign (=) following a rank indicates the same height between two or more buildings. An asterisk (<nowiki>*</nowiki>) indicates that the building is still under construction but has been topped out. The "Year" column indicates the year in which a building was completed.
